The first person controller script for the KinematicBody-node in Godot Engine 3.x is as follows:

    extends KinematicBody
    var speed = 5
    var velocity = Vector3()
    var direction = Vector3()

    func _physics_process(delta):
        direction = Vector3()

        if Input.is_action_pressed("ui_right"):
            direction.x += 1
        if Input.is_action_pressed("ui_left"):
            direction.x -= 1
        if Input.is_action_pressed("ui_up"):
            direction.z += 1
        if Input.is_action_pressed("ui_down"):
            direction.z -= 1

        if direction!= Vector3():
            direction = direction.normalized()

        velocity = direction * speed

        move_and_slide(velocity)


To use this script with the KinematicBody-node in Godot Engine 4.x, simply copy and paste the script into a new script file and attach it to the KinematicBody-node. There is no equivalent to the KinematicBody-node in Godot Engine 4.x, so the same script can be used for any node that inherits from the RigidBody2D or RigidBody3D-node.

In Godot Engine 4.x, there are some changes to the Input class that affect how input events are handled. The Input.is_action_pressed() method has been replaced by the Input.is_action_just_pressed() method. This method returns true if the specified action was just pressed during the current frame, rather than if it is currently pressed. To check if an action is currently pressed, use the Input.is_action_pressed() method. The Input.is_action_released() method has also been added, which returns true if the specified action was just released during the current frame.

Here is the updated version of the first person controller script for Godot Engine 4.x:

    extends RigidBody2D or RigidBody3D
    var speed = 5
    var velocity = Vector3()
    var direction = Vector3()

    func _physics_process(delta):
        direction = Vector3()

        if Input.is_action_just_pressed("ui_right"):
