A table of contents is a list that is generated automatically by the document class. It can be generated with the following commands:

1. \tableofcontents - inserts the table of contents at the beginning of the document. It must be placed after the \begin{document} command.

2. \addcontentsline{toc}{<type>}{<title>} - inserts a new line in the table of contents. The <type> is the sectioning type (e.g. chapter, section, subsection, etc.) and <title> is the title of the section.

3. \label{<label>} - adds a label to the section. The <label> is a unique identifier that can be used to refer to the section.

4. \ref{<label>} - references the section labeled with <label>.

Here is an example of a document with a table of contents:

\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}
\tableofcontents
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
This is the introduction.\label{sec:sec1}
\subsection{First subsection}
\label{sec:subsec1}
This is the first subsection.\label{sec:subsec2}
\subsection{Second subsection}
\label{sec:subsec3}
This is the second subsection.\label{sec:subsec4}
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This is the conclusion.\label{sec:sec2}
\end{document}

The output of this code will be a document with a table of contents at the beginning that looks like this:

1 Introduction.............................. 1
1.1 First subsection....................... 2
1.2 Second subsection..................... 3
2 Conclusion.............................. 4

